  "explanation": "A null hypothesis is a statement hypothesizing that there is no significant difference or relationship between the variables being studied. It is usually written in terms of an equality and serves as a baseline to test statistical significance against. In contrast to the alternative hypothesis which suggests there is an effect or relationship between two measured phenomena, the null hypothesis assumes there’s none, based on observed information.\n\nA p-value represents the probability of observing the results of a given sample or more extreme ones, if the null hypothesis is true. If the p-value is very low (commonly below a significance level like 0.05), it suggests the observed data contradicts the null hypothesis so much that the null might be false, leading us to reject it in favor of the alternative hypothesis. However, if the p-value is not sufficiently low, we retain the null hypothesis because there isn't strong enough evidence to refute it."
